Quite a number of people have been asking for an HP Laserjet/Troff filter.
Here it is.  Sorta.

I have been trying to write one, and have gotten a fair ways, but I've
got to do some "real work", so...  This software is INCOMPLETE, but
does manage to print something sorta useful.  At least it understands
troff output.

Status:
	1) Seems to print normal text "reasonably" well.  The major difficulty
	   is that I don't know how to set up troff width tables, so I
	   ended up having to use CW.  The spacing leaves something to
	   be desired.  Laserjet fonts don't work quite the same way
	   that troff expects (eg: glyph origins etc.)
	2) it only can print Laserjet EPROM fonts.  That is:
		a) pitch selection is limited.  eg: only one.  The lroff
		   shell file overrides the default pitch and turns
		   off ligature mode.
		b) italics and bold work if you have the right font cartridge
		   (I'm using 92286A).
		c) Not all troff characters are possible.  Note: in the
		   mathematics fonts, if you attempt one that isn't supported
		   you will get the "english version".
		d) full fonts may be impossible due to buffer size limitations
		   on the HP Laserjet.
	3) troff does not output page breaks, so the filter has to resort
	   to a kludge to figger out the end of a page (eg: EXACTLY 11 inches
	   of vertical motion).  God help you if you change the page size
	   in troff!  Also, some of the troff macro packages go to the
	   trouble of emitting cut marks.  lcat tries to delete them, but
	   may not always be successful.

Files:
	Makefile (what else)

	lcat.c fastfonts.c (source for lcat filter)

	lroff (a shell file that attempts to emulate troff - you will
	       probably have to change some of the paths)

	test.1 - a sample input for lroff.

	This was built in the System V universe, and lroff depends upon
	System V lp.  "lp -ob -op" means: "no output translations" whatsoever
	(it depends on 8 bit character serial transmission)

Lcat takes one parameter "-d" - VERY VERBOSE debugging output.

I would dearly love to hear from anybody who manages to interface this
durn thing to some sort of font library and/or at least gets the spacing
correct.

I know that this thing is hardly useful, but maybe some troff hackers
can figger out how to extend it.

Enjoy....

